.TH IOM_ABORT 3
.SH NAME
iom_abort \- abort a blocking iom_wait call
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <iom.h>
.PP
.BI "int iom_abort(iomux_t* " c ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iom_abort
signals an I/O multiplexing context
.I c
that it should immediately cease all pending blocking operations.
.PP
When called, this function marks the context as aborted (setting the internal status to -2) and triggers the synchronization primitive (semaphore or condition variable) associated with the context to wake up any threads currently blocked in
.BR iom_wait (3).
.PP
Subsequent calls to
.B iom_wait
using this context will return -2, indicating that the context is no longer in a valid state for multiplexing. This is typically used to perform a clean shutdown of an I/O loop across multiple threads.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B -1
Failure, the semaphore could not be signaled (this is rare and usually indicates a system resource issue).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iom_wait (3),
.BR iom_init (3)